.TH "ctags-lang-odin" "" "" ""
.SH Name
ctags-lang-odin \- 
.sp
Notes about tagging Odin source code with Universal Ctags
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B Version
6.2.0
.TP
.B Manual group
Universal Ctags
.TP
.B Manual section
7
.UNINDENT
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBctags\fP ... \-\-languages=+Odin ...
\fBctags\fP ... \-\-language\-force=Odin ...
\fBctags\fP ... \-\-map\-Odin=+.odin ...
.fi
.sp
.SH DESCRIPTION
.sp
This man page gathers notes about tagging Odin source code
with Universal Ctags.
.sp
The Odin parser tags packages, procedures, constants, variables,
structs, enums, unions, struct members, enum values, type aliases,
and foreign imports.
.SH Distinguishing types from constants
.sp
In Odin, the syntax for a type alias and a constant binding are
identical:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
My_Type :: Other_Type
MY_CONST :: OTHER_CONST
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
When the right\-hand side of a \fB::\fP binding is a plain identifier,
the parser cannot syntactically determine whether it is a type alias
or a constant. In this case, the parser uses a naming\-convention
heuristic: if the name on the left\-hand side contains any lowercase
letter, it is tagged as a \fBtype\fP (kind \fBt\fP); if the name is
entirely uppercase (and underscores/digits), it is tagged as a
\fBconst\fP (kind \fBc\fP).
.sp
This follows the strong Odin convention that type names use
\fBAda_Case\fP and constants use \fBSCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE\fP\&.
.sp
\(dqinput.odin\(dq
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
package example

Handle :: distinct rawptr
Vector2 :: [2]f32
Callback :: proc(x: int) \-> bool
My_Alias :: Some_Type
MAX_SIZE :: SOME_VALUE
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\(dqoutput.tags\(dq
with \(dq\-\-options=NONE \-\-sort=no \-\-fields=+K \-o \- input.odin\(dq
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
example input.odin      /^package example$/;\(dq   package
Handle  input.odin      /^Handle :: distinct rawptr$/;\(dq type    package:example
Vector2 input.odin      /^Vector2 :: [2]f32$/;\(dq type    package:example
Callback        input.odin      /^Callback :: proc(x: int) \-> bool$/;\(dq  type    package:example
My_Alias        input.odin      /^My_Alias :: Some_Type$/;\(dq     type    package:example
MAX_SIZE        input.odin      /^MAX_SIZE :: SOME_VALUE$/;\(dq    const   package:example
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that when the right\-hand side is a keyword such as \fBstruct\fP,
\fBenum\fP, \fBunion\fP, \fBdistinct\fP, \fBproc\fP, \fBmap\fP, or a syntactic
form such as \fB^Type\fP, \fB[N]Type\fP, or \fB#type\fP, the parser
identifies the kind unambiguously without relying on the heuristic.
.sp
The heuristic will misclassify names that break the convention, such
as an all\-uppercase type alias (\fBHANDLE :: Some_Type\fP would be
tagged as \fBconst\fP) or a lowercase\-containing constant
(\fBkMaxSize :: SOME_VALUE\fP would be tagged as \fBtype\fP).
In practice, these cases are rare in idiomatic Odin code.
.SH Foreign blocks
.sp
The parser extracts procedure and variable declarations from
\fBforeign\fP blocks.
.sp
\(dqinput.odin\(dq
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
package ffi

foreign import libc \(dqsystem:libc.so\(dq

foreign libc {
    puts :: proc \(dqc\(dq (s: cstring) \-> i32 \-\-\-
    errno: i32
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\(dqoutput.tags\(dq
with \(dq\-\-options=NONE \-\-sort=no \-\-fields=+K \-o \- input.odin\(dq
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
ffi     input.odin      /^package ffi$/;\(dq       package
libc    input.odin      /^foreign import libc \(dqsystem:libc.so\(dq$/;\(dq      foreign package:ffi
puts    input.odin      /^    puts :: proc \(dqc\(dq (s: cstring) \-> i32 \-\-\-$/;\(dq      proc    package:ffi
errno   input.odin      /^    errno: i32$/;\(dq    var     package:ffi
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH SEE ALSO
.sp
ctags(1), ctags\-client\-tools(7)
